plan:   device-cache-mb 84528      [measured] the experts used most live on the processor that computes them fastest, as the RAM cache holds what flash would read: CUDA0 computes this model's experts at 1635 GiB/s against the host's 371, both measured on this model's own expert block; a cache of 84528 MiB (82620 the capacity fitter gave 12 of 40 layers' experts, 1908 it left free under one more whole layer) serves 89% of a token's routed bytes (from this model's own routing); a miss is uploaded from the host
plan:   ubatch         0          [derived] a graph as wide as the context reserves 199 MiB of compute buffers, inside 10% of the 268977 MiB this process may hold: a prompt is computed in one pass
plan:   placement      41 layers on devices [measured] the capacity fitter found a placement that is projected to fit; 40 of 40 MoE layers keep their experts on the host, host-resident 199 MiB, on devices 11141 MiB, context 2048 (fitter's host breakdown: model 386859, context 0, compute 199 MiB; file 477506, experts 275400)
plan:   ctx-size       2048       [policy] the value this run was given, not a choice: the capacity fitter only picks a context when it is handed a zero and this planner always pins it, so nothing has weighed this number against what the memory it reserves would buy as expert cache
plan:   regime         experts-stream [derived] on the host 275599 MiB (dense 199) against 215182 MiB usable, which is the 268977 MiB this process may hold (as reported available, unmeasured) less a 53795 MiB margin (the process is killed)
plan:   extra-offload  none       [policy] the fitter has already placed what fits on the devices - see the placement line above - and placing weights across devices is a capacity problem it solves exactly; this planner owns the tier below it and does not second-guess it
plan:   experts        host       [derived] this device could compute them on paper - it offers a host buffer and runs the model's own layout - and it still cannot. The streamer rebinds every expert onto memory it reserved itself, which no device was given access to; and a host buffer type would not survive the load anyway, since a mapped model has one substituted for the CPU's. Whether this device can wrap memory a caller already owns - the one opening left - is unprobed. What it advertises is a blanket promise, and a backend whose support is conditional declines to make one while implementing the call anyway, so the absence of a yes decides nothing here
plan:   dense-weights  anon       [derived] no reclaim-exempt store here, so anonymous buffers are what keeps the dense set off a flash refault (a reclaim then means: the process is killed)
plan:   prefill-device off        [derived] a device expert cache is set, which keeps the layers on their device and computes the experts there; a prefill device is not combined with it
plan:   cache-pin      off        [derived] not even one token cycle of the cache can be locked in place here after what is ahead of it, so the cache is ordinary memory and no lock is asked for
plan:   memory-ledger  215181 MiB [derived] held 215181 of 215182 MiB holdable, of which locked 0 MiB of 0 MiB lockable: context and compute 199, expert cache 214982
plan:   storage-rate   5.3 GiB/s  [derived] this model's expert slice is 6120 KiB and at that request size this storage gives 5380 MiB/s over 4 lanes: the expert lane is priced there, not at the storage's peak
plan:   cache-unpriced 209.9 GiB  [derived] the expert cache was sized from what remains after the dense policy (214982 MiB) rather than from what a hit is worth: this machine's read rate was not measured, so there is no price to rank it against. It is the answer that was available, not the answer that was chosen.
plan:   device-unapplied -          [derived] a device won at least one group, but this build computes on the host: the placement is reported, not applied
plan:   cache-hits     91%        [measured] measured on this model: an LRU replay of the routing it recorded on an earlier run (256 decoded and 26 prompt tokens) at every cache size up to the whole expert set; at 214982 MiB it serves 91% of the decode lookups, where the linear credit would have said 78%
plan:   moe-stream     on         [derived] the experts do not fit but the dense set does
plan:   cache-mb       214982     [derived] what the cache is worth out of 215182 MiB usable, ranked against the resident groups by seconds saved per byte of RAM, with what none of them wanted added: 417088 MiB committed in total, of which 214982 MiB is cache, over a floor of 4303 MiB
plan:   io-threads     4          [measured] the highest measured rate for this model's 6120 KiB expert slice on this storage
plan:   o-direct       on         [measured] uncached reads return correct bytes on this path
plan:   release-mmap   off        [unprobed] the storage probe did not see a live mapping slow its reads here (5224 vs 6064 MiB/s), but it is an instrument validated only in the positive direction, so that is not evidence there is nothing to gain: try --release-mmap and measure
plan:   lossy          off        [policy] no quality budget was given, and a policy that changes the output must never arm itself
